{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-8188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-9+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $1$, find the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7df6c006c50cfccd77609d1a99502785fcfd188fb360a296f9a0f75b186f231"} {"question" "q-8188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-9+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-9-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(-9+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}))(x-(-9-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(-9+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})-x(-9-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})+(-9+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})(-9-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9x-x\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}+9x+x\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}+81-2{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+18x+81+2 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+18x+83 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7df6c006c50cfccd77609d1a99502785fcfd188fb360a296f9a0f75b186f231"} {"question" "q-8188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dafe83eaf8868035f5312a4e33b67670947f789078beb71c2edc039decdc57da"} {"question" "q-8188" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7df6c006c50cfccd77609d1a99502785fcfd188fb360a296f9a0f75b186f231"} {"question" "q-8188" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{x}^{2}+18x+79=0"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+18x+83=0"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+9x+79=0"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-18x+83=0"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-9x+79=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7df6c006c50cfccd77609d1a99502785fcfd188fb360a296f9a0f75b186f231"} {"question" "q-8188" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}+18x+83=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7df6c006c50cfccd77609d1a99502785fcfd188fb360a296f9a0f75b186f231"} {"question" "q-28219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=2-\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $2$, find the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7df6c006c50cfccd77609d1a99502785fcfd188fb360a296f9a0f75b186f231"} {"question" "q-28219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=2-\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=2+\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(2-\\text{i}))(x-(2+\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(2+\\text{i})-x(2-\\text{i})+(2-\\text{i})(2+\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x-x\\text{i}-2x+x\\text{i}+4-{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=2\\pm \\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $2.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-4x+5) & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-8x+10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7df6c006c50cfccd77609d1a99502785fcfd188fb360a296f9a0f75b186f231"} {"question" "q-28219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dafe83eaf8868035f5312a4e33b67670947f789078beb71c2edc039decdc57da"} {"question" "q-28219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7df6c006c50cfccd77609d1a99502785fcfd188fb360a296f9a0f75b186f231"} {"question" "q-28219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{x}^{2}+8x-10=0"] ["math" "2{x}^{2}-2x+10=0"] ["math" "2{x}^{2}-4x+5=0"] ["math" "2{x}^{2}-8x+10=0"] ["math" "2{x}^{2}-8x+5=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7df6c006c50cfccd77609d1a99502785fcfd188fb360a296f9a0f75b186f231"} {"question" "q-28219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{x}^{2}-8x+10=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7df6c006c50cfccd77609d1a99502785fcfd188fb360a296f9a0f75b186f231"} {"question" "q-160006" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $4$, find the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7df6c006c50cfccd77609d1a99502785fcfd188fb360a296f9a0f75b186f231"} {"question" "q-160006" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=-\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:  \n\n$$\n(x-\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i})(x+\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i})=0\n$$\n\n   \n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $4.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $4.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4}) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+5 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7df6c006c50cfccd77609d1a99502785fcfd188fb360a296f9a0f75b186f231"} {"question" "q-160006" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dafe83eaf8868035f5312a4e33b67670947f789078beb71c2edc039decdc57da"} {"question" "q-160006" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7df6c006c50cfccd77609d1a99502785fcfd188fb360a296f9a0f75b186f231"} {"question" "q-160006" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4{x}^{2}+2=0"] ["math" "4{x}^{2}+3=0"] ["math" "4{x}^{2}+5=0"] ["math" "4{x}^{2}-2=0"] ["math" "4{x}^{2}-3=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7df6c006c50cfccd77609d1a99502785fcfd188fb360a296f9a0f75b186f231"} {"question" "q-160006" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4{x}^{2}+5=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7df6c006c50cfccd77609d1a99502785fcfd188fb360a296f9a0f75b186f231"} {"question" "q-160008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=2+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $2$, find the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7df6c006c50cfccd77609d1a99502785fcfd188fb360a296f9a0f75b186f231"} {"question" "q-160008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=2+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=2-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(2+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}))(x-(2-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(2-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})-x(2+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})+(2+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})(2-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+\\sqrt{3}x\\text{i}-2x-\\sqrt{3}x\\text{i}+4-(\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+3 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+7 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=2\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $2.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-4x+7) & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-8x+14 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7df6c006c50cfccd77609d1a99502785fcfd188fb360a296f9a0f75b186f231"} {"question" "q-160008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dafe83eaf8868035f5312a4e33b67670947f789078beb71c2edc039decdc57da"} {"question" "q-160008" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7df6c006c50cfccd77609d1a99502785fcfd188fb360a296f9a0f75b186f231"} {"question" "q-160008" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{x}^{2}+16x+14=0"] ["math" "2{x}^{2}+8x+7=0"] ["math" "2{x}^{2}-16x-14=0"] ["math" "2{x}^{2}-4x+7=0"] ["math" "2{x}^{2}-8x+14=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7df6c006c50cfccd77609d1a99502785fcfd188fb360a296f9a0f75b186f231"} {"question" "q-160008" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{x}^{2}-8x+14=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7df6c006c50cfccd77609d1a99502785fcfd188fb360a296f9a0f75b186f231"} {"question" "q-274994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-2\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $10,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7df6c006c50cfccd77609d1a99502785fcfd188fb360a296f9a0f75b186f231"} {"question" "q-274994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-2\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=2\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-2\\text{i})(x+2\\text{i})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm 2\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $10.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $10.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10({x}^{2}+4) & =0 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}+40 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7df6c006c50cfccd77609d1a99502785fcfd188fb360a296f9a0f75b186f231"} {"question" "q-274994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "dafe83eaf8868035f5312a4e33b67670947f789078beb71c2edc039decdc57da"} {"question" "q-274994" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7df6c006c50cfccd77609d1a99502785fcfd188fb360a296f9a0f75b186f231"} {"question" "q-274994" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10x^2+40"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040736/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7df6c006c50cfccd77609d1a99502785fcfd188fb360a296f9a0f75b186f231"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1456 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
